1
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:03,660
Oh, look,  Saturn 3 is on.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,750
I don't want to watch  Saturn 3.

3
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:07,150
Deep Space Nine is better.

4
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,920
How is Deep Space Nine better than Saturn 3?

5
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:14,680
Simple subtraction will tell you it's six better.

6
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:19,580
Compromise. Watch Babylon 5.

7
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:22,650
In what sense is that a compromise?

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:27,350
Well, five is partway between three... Never mind.

9
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:28,480
I'll tell you what.

10
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:30,180
How about we go rock-paper-scissors?

11
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:31,420
Ooh, I don't think so.

12
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,250
Anecdotal evidence suggests

13
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:34,850
that in the game of rock-paper-scissors,

14
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:38,220
players familiar with each other will tie 75 to 80% of the time

15
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:40,650
due to the limited number of outcomes.

16
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:45,020
I suggest rock-paper-scissors-lizard-Spock.

17
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:46,650
What?

18
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,450
It's very simple.

19
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:51,020
Scissors cuts paper. Paper covers rock.

20
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,380
Rock crushes lizard.

21
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:53,650
Lizard poisons Spock.

22
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:56,550
Spock smashes scissors. Scissors decapitates lizard.

23
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,080
Lizard eats paper. Paper disproves Spock.

24
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:00,820
Spock vaporizes rock.

25
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:03,980
And as it always has, rock crushes scissors.

26
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,620
Okay, I think I got it.

27
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:12,620
Rock-paper-scissors- lizard-Spock!

28
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,050
Hello, boys.

29
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:25,950
Ahoy, matey.

30
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,080
Noticed the eye patch, did you?

31
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:30,180
It's all part of a technique I've been studying

32
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:31,780
for picking up women.

33
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:33,580
You employ a visual display

34
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:35,420
designed to make yourself

35
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:38,420
distinctive and memorable.

36
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,050
Oh, yes, like the male peacock with brilliant plumage

37
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:44,650
or the rutting baboon with engorged hindquarters.

38
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:48,480
Or in this case, the bar mitzvah boy with pinkeye.

39
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:51,180
Mock me if you will, but it works.

40
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:53,250
You show up at a club in something distinctive,

41
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,980
scope out your target and toss out some negs.

42
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:56,980
What are negs?

43
00:01:57,050 --> 00:01:58,450
A neg is a negative compliment

44
00:01:58,520 --> 00:02:00,420
that throws a pretty woman off her game,

45
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:03,820
like "Normally, I'm not turned on by big teeth,

46
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:06,650
but on you, they work."

47
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:09,780
I got a whole list of 'em. Who wants to be my wingman?

48
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:11,120
You're not gonna need wingman.

49
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:13,980
You're gonna need a paramedic.

50
00:02:14,050 --> 00:02:15,920
Howard, your scooter's blocking my car.

51
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:19,820
Aw, did you get pinkeye again?

52
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:23,780
Step one: she notices the eye patch.

53
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:26,150
May I say, Penny,

54
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:29,120
not a lot of women could look as hot as you do

55
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,280
with such greasy hair?

56
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,250
Yeah, just move your stupid scooter

57
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,620
before I pick it up and throw it in the dumpster.

58
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:46,650
Ow.

59
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:17,720
I'm sorry, but I'm not going to watch The Clone Wars TV series

60
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,950
until I've seen  The e Clone Wars movie.

61
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:22,780
I prefer to let George Lucas disappoint me

62
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:25,450
in the order he intended.

63
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:28,320
Well, I want to watch it now.

64
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,280
Then I believe we've arrived at another quintessential

65
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:34,520
rock-paper-scissors- lizard-Spock moment.

66
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:41,850
Watch whatever you want.

67
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:44,050
I saw what you did there.

68
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:46,580
What'd I do?

69
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:53,620
Hello. Hey, Howard.

70
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:54,850
What's wrong?

71
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:58,150
Okay. Okay, we'll be right there.

72
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:00,320
What happened?

73
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:02,380
Howard's at the Mars Rover lab. He says he's in trouble.

74
00:04:02,450 --> 00:04:03,450
Defcon 5.

75
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:04,920
Defcon 5?

76
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:07,520
Well, there's no need to rush.

77
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:08,680
What?

78
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:10,320
Defcon 5 means "no danger."

79
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:12,520
Defcon 1 is a crisis.

80
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,680
How can 5 not be worse than 1?

81
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:18,420
Yeah, Star Trek V worse than I.

82
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:21,020
Okay, first of all, that's a comparison of quality,

83
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:22,380
not intensity.

84
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:24,850
Secondly, Star Trek I is orders-of-magnitude worse

85
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:26,550
than Star Trek V.

86
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:28,980
Are you joking?  Star Trek V is the standard

87
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:31,020
against which all badness is measured.

88
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:32,180
No, no.

89
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:34,720
Star Trek V has specific failures

90
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:35,980
in writing and direction,

91
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:37,650
while Star Trek I fails across the board:

92
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:41,450
art direction, costuming, music, sound editing.

93
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,780
Can we just forget I said Defcon and go?

94
00:04:45,450 --> 00:04:48,250
Star Trek V.

95
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:51,180
All right, will you at least stipulate

96
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:54,250
that Star Trek IV:  The Voyage Home is inarguably the best?

97
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:56,450
I have three words for you:

98
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:57,950
Wrath of Khan.

99
00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:59,950
Oh, thank God, you're here.

100
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:01,180
What's the emergency?

101
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:04,450
I got the Mars Rover stuck in a ditch.

102
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:05,580
Where?

103
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:07,750
On a dusty highway just outside Bakersfield.

104
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:09,650
Where do you think? On Mars!

105
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,350
Howard, is everything okay?

106
00:05:12,410 --> 00:05:14,850
Yeah, baby, I'll be right in.

107
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:19,010
You brought a girl to the Mars Rover control room?

108
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:21,350
Yeah, I picked her up in the bar. She's a doctor.

109
00:05:21,410 --> 00:05:23,180
One free barium enema,

110
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:25,550
and my mother won't care she's not Jewish.

111
00:05:25,610 --> 00:05:27,680
Wait a minute.

112
00:05:27,750 --> 00:05:29,650
So the eye patch and e insults worked?

113
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:32,010
No, there were three other guys with eye patches.

114
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,310
It was a fiasco.

115
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:37,380
What did work was, "How'd you like to visit

116
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:38,810
a secret government facility?"

117
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,980
So what exactly do you want us to do?

118
00:05:41,050 --> 00:05:43,210
I need you and Raj to help me get the Rover out of the ditch

119
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:44,510
and I need you to get Stephanie out

120
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:45,580
before somebody notices she's here.

121
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:47,150
She doesn't exactly have clearance.

122
00:05:47,210 --> 00:05:49,880
Really? They don't let strange women from honky-tonks come in

123
00:05:49,950 --> 00:05:52,110
and play with $200 million government projects

124
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:53,310
on distant planets?

125
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:55,980
Yes, I was bad. Maybe she'll spank me.

126
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:57,510
Can we please move on?

127
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:00,710
Hey, Howard, you know, it's getting late,

128
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:02,880
so do I get to drive this thing or what?

129
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:05,750
Yeah. No. I'm sorry, but something's come up.

130
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:08,750
Kind of a Mars Rover... Mars Rover

131
00:06:08,810 --> 00:06:13,110
can Howard come over...

132
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:14,980
situation.

133
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:19,650
So my friend Leonard is gonna take you home.

134
00:06:19,710 --> 00:06:22,310
Oh, okay. Let's go, friend Leonard.

135
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:24,350
I'll call ya.

136
00:06:24,410 --> 00:06:26,410
Yeah. So are you a scientist like Howard?

137
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:29,810
No one's a scientist like Howard.

138
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,980
My mother is so gonna love her.

139
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:34,650
How nice. Maybe they can carpool

140
00:06:34,710 --> 00:06:36,250
when they visit you in federal prison.

141
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:46,180
I'm sorry. I totally interrupted you.

142
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:47,580
What, what, what were you saying?

143
00:06:47,650 --> 00:06:49,180
Just said Howard's a terrific guy.

144
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:54,350
He's got a great sense of humor.

145
00:06:54,410 --> 00:06:55,980
He loves his mother...

146
00:06:56,050 --> 00:06:57,250
a lot.

147
00:06:57,310 --> 00:06:59,010
People say "too much."

148
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,910
I really like that you're such a loyal friend.

149
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,710
Yeah, I am loyal.

150
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:06,510
You know, if you look at the big picture.

151
00:07:11,450 --> 00:07:12,810
Out of curiosity,

152
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,550
did he ever have a shot with you?

153
00:07:14,610 --> 00:07:15,550
Are you insane?

154
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:18,080
The guy was wearing an eye patch.

155
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:19,450
Then why did you...?

156
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:22,950
He said that I cou drive a car on Mars.

157
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:25,080
Got it.

158
00:07:27,410 --> 00:07:29,380
So, can I see you again?

159
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:31,780
You're not gonna see me now.

160
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:36,810
Cool.

161
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:42,880
Anything?

162
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:45,580
Actually, I was just checking my e-mail.

163
00:07:47,050 --> 00:07:49,950
But, uh, no, the Rover is not responding.

164
00:07:50,010 --> 00:07:51,950
I believe the appropriate metaphor here

165
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:53,480
involves a river of excrement

166
00:07:53,550 --> 00:07:55,180
and a Native American water vessel

167
00:07:55,250 --> 00:07:57,780
without any means of propulsion.

168
00:07:57,850 --> 00:07:58,750
Hang on.

169
00:07:58,810 --> 00:08:00,380
There's got to be other options.

170
00:08:00,450 --> 00:08:02,880
You could try calling Triple-A.

171
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:04,880
But based on NASA's latest timetable,

172
00:08:04,950 --> 00:08:06,850
they won't get there for 35 years.

173
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:09,850
Plus I understand you have to be standing

174
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:12,610
next to the vehicle with your card when they arrive.

175
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,850
Oh, snap.

176
00:08:14,910 --> 00:08:17,150
Snap what?

177
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:19,550
Okay, I guess we have to turn to Plan B.

178
00:08:19,610 --> 00:08:20,810
What's Plan B?

179
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,410
Erase all the hard drives, scrap the surveillance tapes,

180
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,210
wipe our fingerprints off every surface and run.

181
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:27,110
Why wasn'that Plan A?

182
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:32,850
A NASA spokesman states that due to the loss of data,

183
00:08:32,910 --> 00:08:37,610
they will most likely be unable to determine the cause of the Mars Rover's malfunction.

184
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:40,410
This is not the first time an exploratory mission to Mars

185
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:41,710
has ended in disappointment.

186
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:44,950
Thank God for Plan B.

187
00:08:45,310 --> 00:08:47,210
Howard, didn't you say you worked on the Mars Rover?

188
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:49,750
No, you're mistaken.

189
00:08:49,810 --> 00:08:52,610
Yeah, when we first met, you said that if I went out with you,

190
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:54,510
I could drive a car on Mars.

191
00:08:56,110 --> 00:08:59,980
I don't know what you're talking about.

192
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:04,310
Will you all excuse me?

193
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:05,610
Leonard is subtly signaling

194
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,150
that he'd like to talk to me in private.

195
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:11,950
No. Yeah, I remember specifically.

196
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:13,880
You started by asking if I was from Mars

197
00:09:13,950 --> 00:09:17,180
because my ass was out of this world.

198
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:21,780
Well, that does sound like me, but no.

199
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:24,110
Is there some problem?

200
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:25,280
Yeah.

201
00:09:25,350 --> 00:09:28,310
Listen, I have to kinda sneak out for a while.

202
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:29,880
All right, good-bye.

203
00:09:29,950 --> 00:09:31,780
No, wait!

204
00:09:31,850 --> 00:09:34,050
If anyone asks you where I went, you don't know.

205
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:36,050
Where ARE you going?

206
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:37,610
I can't tell you that.

207
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:40,110
Who would ask me?

208
00:09:40,110 --> 00:09:43,280
I can't tell you that, either.

209
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:45,410
So you brought me in here to inform me

210
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:47,180
that you can't tell me where you're going

211
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:50,040
and you can't tell me who might ask?

212
00:09:50,110 --> 00:09:54,310
Yeah, I really didn't think this through.

213
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:56,680
Leonard, a moment.

214
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:58,240
What?

215
00:09:58,310 --> 00:10:01,180
If someone-- and of course, we don't know who this would be--

216
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,340
does ask where you've gone, what should I say?

217
00:10:03,410 --> 00:10:05,740
I don't know. Just tell 'em I went to the office.

218
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:07,680
Are you going to the office? No.

219
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:09,680
Then how can I say it convincingly?

220
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:12,040
Just say, "Leonard went to the office."

221
00:10:12,110 --> 00:10:16,440
All right. Leonard went to the office.

222
00:10:20,110 --> 00:10:22,410
What is...? No, not like that!

223
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:24,840
Just, "Leonard went to the office."

224
00:10:24,910 --> 00:10:26,240
This would have worked out a lot better

225
00:10:26,310 --> 00:10:28,340
if you'd just told me you were going to the office.

226
00:10:28,410 --> 00:10:29,710
I'm going to the office.

227
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:32,510
See? Why don't I believe you?

228
00:10:37,210 --> 00:10:38,910
I'm going out for a while.

229
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:40,780
Okay.

230
00:10:49,210 --> 00:10:53,040
Doesn't anyone want to know where he's going?

231
00:10:53,110 --> 00:10:56,380
Okay, where is he going?

232
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:58,740
Leonard is going to the office.

233
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:15,470
So how was work today?

234
00:11:15,530 --> 00:11:17,000
Busy.

235
00:11:17,070 --> 00:11:18,600
I removed an appendix, a gall bladder

236
00:11:18,670 --> 00:11:20,680
and about a foot and a half of bowel.

237
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,400
I'm hoping that's three different guys.

238
00:11:25,470 --> 00:11:28,000
No, just the one.

239
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:30,170
He didn't make it.

240
00:11:32,470 --> 00:11:33,900
So, how was your day?

241
00:11:33,970 --> 00:11:36,770
Oh, you know, I'm a physicist, so...

242
00:11:36,830 --> 00:11:39,300
I thought about stuff.

243
00:11:39,370 --> 00:11:40,970
That's it?

244
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,300
Well, I wrote some of it down.

245
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:46,900
Are you done eating?

246
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:48,930
Uh, yeah. Oh, good.

247
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:53,730
If I knew you were waiting,

248
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:56,160
I would've swallowed that lasagna whole.

249
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:00,560
You've reached Dr. Stephanie Barnett.

250
00:12:00,630 --> 00:12:02,730
Leave a message.

251
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:04,230
Hey, Steph,

252
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:05,260
it's me again, Howard.

253
00:12:05,330 --> 00:12:06,800
Listen, if you're free Friday,

254
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:09,600
maybe we could have a little something to eat at my place.

255
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:12,000
My mom cooks a hell of a brisket.

256
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:13,830
Let me know.

257
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,330
It's Howard.

258
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:19,730
I've had her brisket. Melts in your mouth.

259
00:12:24,230 --> 00:12:26,760
Maybe we should think about going to the bedroom.

260
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:28,000
That's a good idea.

261
00:12:28,060 --> 00:12:29,830
There's a bed in there, and I'm very...

262
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:31,860
very, very pro-bed.

263
00:12:34,390 --> 00:12:36,560
You've reached Dr. Stephanie Barnett.

264
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:39,160
Leave a message.

265
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:41,260
Hey, it's me again.

266
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,120
Just want to let you know

267
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:45,960
the head count for dinner Friday has gone up.

268
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:49,760
My Aunt Betty and Uncle Elliot are coming in from Palm Springs.

269
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,390
Oh, and if anybody should ask,

270
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,260
you're half-Jewish on your mother's side.

271
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:57,460
Okay, call me.

272
00:12:57,520 --> 00:12:59,990
It's Howard.

273
00:13:00,060 --> 00:13:03,260
Don't you think we should tell him you're not interested?

274
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:07,160
Do you want me to stop and call him back right now?

275
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:08,720
Dear God, no!

276
00:13:11,420 --> 00:13:13,620
Oh, Leonard...

277
00:13:16,390 --> 00:13:18,560
You've reached Dr. Stephanie Barnett.

278
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:20,460
Leave a message.

279
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:23,890
Hey, it's me again. Howard.

280
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:28,860
Listen, my cousins from Fort Lauderdale are flying in to meet you

281
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:32,960
so that means we're gonna have to move the dinner to a restaurant.

282
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:36,460
Tell her we're going to the Olive Garden!

283
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:39,820
I have a coupon from the paper.

284
00:13:39,890 --> 00:13:45,120
We're not going to the Olive Garden, Mom!

285
00:13:45,190 --> 00:13:49,120
Oh, Mr. Bigshot with his Red Lobster.

286
00:13:49,190 --> 00:13:54,020
I'll call you back when we firm up the details.

287
00:13:54,090 --> 00:13:56,560
It's Howard.

288
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:03,760
New shirts?

289
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:04,920
Yeah, a couple.

290
00:14:04,990 --> 00:14:06,620
Nice. Thank you.

291
00:14:06,690 --> 00:14:08,590
So, who's the girl?

292
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:10,760
I'm sorry?

293
00:14:10,820 --> 00:14:13,690
Well, last time you bought a new shirt was when we were dating.

294
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:16,990
So what we did was in fact dating?

295
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:20,560
Well, yeah, we did have a date.

296
00:14:20,620 --> 00:14:22,860
Exactly. Thank you.

297
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:27,290
Do me a favor. Tell Koothrappali that next time you see him.

298
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:29,560
So, who is she?

299
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:31,790
Oh, she's a doctor.

300
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:33,190
Oh, nice.

301
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:36,760
A doctor doctor, or a you kind of doctor?

302
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:37,860
Doctor doctor.

303
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:39,590
Surgical resident.

304
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:41,090
Smart, pretty.

305
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:44,760
Let me ask you something.

306
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:48,790
If your friend thinks he's dating someone,

307
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:51,720
but he's not because, in fact, you're dating her,

308
00:14:51,790 --> 00:14:53,390
does that make you a bad person?

309
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:54,690
Well, that depends.

310
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:55,690
On what?

311
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:57,960
Is that friend Wolowitz?

312
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:00,260
Yeah.

313
00:15:00,330 --> 00:15:01,760
Screw him. You're fine.

314
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:05,730
Are you sure?

315
00:15:05,800 --> 00:15:08,060
Well, have you slept with her yet?

316
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:12,730
You dog!

317
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:14,530
Good for you.

318
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:16,330
Does that change things?

319
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:17,600
No.

320
00:15:17,660 --> 00:15:19,860
So why'd you ask?

321
00:15:19,930 --> 00:15:22,330
I'm nosy. See ya.

322
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:30,360
Howard, get the door!

323
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:34,600
Really? Is that what you do when someone knocks?!

324
00:15:34,660 --> 00:15:38,030
Thank you. I had no idea!

325
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:43,160
Hey, buddy. What brings you to my little slice of hell?

326
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:45,130
Who is it?!

327
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:46,800
It's Leonard!

328
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:49,860
You're gonna have to play outside!

329
00:15:49,930 --> 00:15:53,030
I'm not dressed to receive!

330
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:55,600
No one cares, Ma!

331
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:57,660
So, what's up?

332
00:15:57,730 --> 00:16:00,500
Listen, I need to talk to you about something.

333
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:04,460
Momentito.

334
00:16:04,530 --> 00:16:06,430
Yello.

335
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:07,730
Oh, Stephanie,

336
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,200
thanks for calling me back. I was worried...

337
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:24,460
No, I understand.

338
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:26,400
Sure, we can be friends. Absolutely.

339
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:28,430
Thanks for calling.

340
00:16:28,500 --> 00:16:30,260
Yeah, you have a nice day, too.

341
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:33,560
You are dead to me.

342
00:16:39,410 --> 00:16:41,840
Okay, who gets the extra dumpling?

343
00:16:46,610 --> 00:16:49,110
Rock-paper-scissors- lizard-Spock.

344
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:57,010
Okay, one of us is going to have to stop putting up Spock.

345
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:00,040
How do we decide that? Rock-paper-scissors- lizard-Spock.

346
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:04,380
Oh, hey, guys.

347
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:05,740
Hello.

348
00:17:05,810 --> 00:17:08,210
Hi, Howard.

349
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:12,040
Howard?

350
00:17:12,110 --> 00:17:13,440
Sheldon.

351
00:17:13,510 --> 00:17:17,540
Howard is employing a schoolyard paradigm in which you are,

352
00:17:17,610 --> 00:17:20,010
for all intents and purposes, deceased.

353
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:23,250
He intends to act on this by not speaking to you,

354
00:17:23,250 --> 00:17:25,720
feigning an inability to hear you when you speak

355
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:28,620
and otherwise refusing to acknowledge your existence.

356
00:17:28,680 --> 00:17:29,980
That's just ridiculous.

357
00:17:30,050 --> 00:17:31,620
Why are you cooperating with him?

358
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:34,620
I don't make the rules, Leonard.

359
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:38,280
Howard, come on. I didn't plan on this.

360
00:17:38,350 --> 00:17:40,150
These things just happen.

361
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:42,850
Usually not to me, but they do happen.

362
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:46,850
Did someone just feel a cold breeze?

363
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:49,780
I believe this is an extension of the death metaphor.

364
00:17:51,150 --> 00:17:53,620
The cold breeze is the so-called ectoplasmic issue

365
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:55,220
of a disembodied soul passing by.

366
00:17:56,580 --> 00:18:00,250
You know, screw it. I'm just gonna eat the dumpling.

367
00:18:03,180 --> 00:18:05,320
Hey. Oh, hi, Steph.

368
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:07,380
Come on in. Is this a bad time?

369
00:18:07,450 --> 00:18:09,720
Yeah, but I don't see a better one on the horizon, so...

370
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:14,750
Oh, if it isn't Mrs. Dead to Me.

371
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:16,350
Hello, Howard.

372
00:18:16,420 --> 00:18:17,880
Sheldon?

373
00:18:17,950 --> 00:18:20,180
I'm sorry, you violated the terms of your metaphor

374
00:18:20,250 --> 00:18:22,820
by acknowledging her existence. I'm out.

375
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:25,420
He just won't listen to me.

376
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:28,280
Okay, I guess it'll just be the three of us then.

377
00:18:28,350 --> 00:18:30,720
Lisa's gonna be disappointed. Yeah.

378
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:33,080
Lisa?

379
00:18:33,150 --> 00:18:34,650
Yeah, my roommate.

380
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:36,480
She just went throh a really bad breakup

381
00:18:36,550 --> 00:18:39,120
and I thought she might like to meet somebody fun like you.

382
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:44,900
Leonard, Stephanie, you're alive! It's a miracle.

383
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:49,880
So, anyway, Lisa, I just wanted to tell you again

384
00:18:49,950 --> 00:18:51,880
how much I enjoyed the other night,

385
00:18:51,950 --> 00:18:54,620
and again, I'm sorry for how it ended.

386
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:58,350
But again, if you could let me know about Friday.

387
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:00,050
My mother needs a head count

388
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:02,980
so she can know how big a brisket to get.

389
00:19:03,050 --> 00:19:05,250
Howard, Howard, look at this.

390
00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:07,150
Anyway, call me.

391
00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:09,280
The possibility of life on Mars has long fascinated scientists It's Howard.

392
00:19:09,350 --> 00:19:10,720
and laypersons alike.

393
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:12,850
It's unclear how the Mars Rover got into the crevice,

394
00:19:12,920 --> 00:19:14,550
but one thing's certain:

395
00:19:14,620 --> 00:19:16,350
The data which it has sent back contain

396
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:18,720
the first clear indications

397
00:19:18,780 --> 00:19:22,020
that there may have been life on Mars.

398
00:19:22,050 --> 00:19:27,780
It's a scientific discovery that has staggering implications for all mankind.

399
00:19:27,850 --> 00:19:31,650
Unfortunately, we'll never know who's responsible.

400
00:19:37,050 --> 00:19:39,680
Son of a bitch.

